Yes, but their role has evolved
; traditional physical answering machines are less common due to smartphones, but the core function of "store-and-forward" communication lives on as ubiquitous voicemail, text, and AI-driven services, meeting the continued need for capturing messages when people can't answer in real-time, though many prefer texting over leaving voicemails now. 
Why people still "need" them (in updated forms)

    Voicemail & Texting: The fundamental need for off-line communication (voice or text) persists for personal and business use when calls aren't answered immediately, replacing physical devices.
    Business Continuity: For businesses, modern solutions offer 24/7 message capture, call screening, and personalized greetings, preventing lost opportunities.
    Remote Access: Digital voicemail allows checking messages from anywhere, unlike older machines requiring physical presence, as explained in this HowStuffWorks article. 

Why traditional machines declined

    Cell Phones: Mobile devices made landline answering machines less necessary as people carried their communication with them.
    Texting Culture: For many, sending a quick text is now preferred over leaving a voicemail, especially for informal communication, as discussed on Reddit.
    Landline Phase-Out: Traditional copper landlines are being replaced by digital systems, making older tech redundant, according to iFax. 

Modern replacements & alternatives

    VoIP & Cloud Services: Businesses use sophisticated digital systems (VoIP) with email integration (voicemail-to-email) and advanced routing, as noted in this Reddit thread.
    AI Answering Services: Advanced AI provides natural-sounding conversations, handling inquiries without a human, as detailed by Dialbox. 

In essence, while standalone answering machines are largely obsolete, the function of taking messages for later retrieval remains essential, just delivered through smarter, more integrated digital tools. 